    Remove "support" for non-zero array bases

    Got my hands on Perl 5.20.3, and as it turns out,
    $[ works VERY differently than the other globals
    ($., $@, $/, ...) even when it was not illegal...
    at least I am led to believe, the magic $[ (arybase)
    or Array::Base has is limited to its lexical scope
    via $^H{'$['} or $^H{'Array::Base/base'}, and it has
    possibly been this way since Day 1 of Perl 5.  I could
    patch it so that "_parse" reads from (caller(2))[10],
    see if any of these has been set, and offset every
    index by it.  But how would people go about writing
    helper functions for this?  If $[ can't even survive
    to my call stack because I didn't set $[ = 1 in my
    library code at compile-time (with use/BEGIN), and
    now I have to use some wacky run-time code just to add
    this offset to every index I have here, what makes it
    any easier for users of 1-based (or any other non-zero
    base) arrays to define their own libraries?  This
    behavior would be so fragile, were we to propagate,
    because if any step of the chain DOES NOT we won't
    see the ultimate caller's $[.  And I'm sure such an
    unnatural practice is "too much" even when given
    Perl's TIMTOWTDI....

    On the plus side if $[ and Array::Base are trapped in
    their lexical scope, we'd know with confidence that
    we are working with 0-based index regardless of what
    goes on outside (unless someone uses tied array with
    FETCH defined from that scope, which probably needs
    some more explicit documentation... (or not; I think
    in such a case it would be the user's responsiblity
    to add $[ from their scope to the index we return to
    them ($[ from our scope would just always be 0, so...
    better keep it that way I'd say.)  (though i do want
    to see if $[ also affects closure defined within the
    lexical scope where array base is different (since
    our whole module is built around that)???  code like

        bisectl { $array[$_] <=> $x } $[, $#array+1;

    could still break, and thus might warrant another,
    separate caveat note...))

    either way... $[ is just going away. >:(  which means
    hard-coded 0 for left search and -1 for right search!
    (but that's probably for the better... (an array index
    is SSize_t in Perl anyways -- there is actually much
    less harm in going below 0 than I previously believed,
    so we don't really have to "avoid" it anymore...))

